WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:09.530
My name is Nicolás Rojas Castro. I’m an&nbsp;independent
filmmaker from Chile. And I knew pretty early

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:16.860
on that I wanted to tell stories, hopefully
make movies, I was like 13 I guess.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:22.230
And I went through high school and everything
and then I went to a pretty humble university

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:31.410
called Viña del Mar University where I studied
cinema (it’s like a film school) and I had

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:39.399
pretty good teachers (like screenwriting teachers)
and history teachers and cinema teachers who

00:00:39.399 --> 00:00:47.850
taught you more the craft than to appreciate
the art itself. And in doing my University

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:55.300
days, I made a couple of short films and I
ended up in my career with a very ambitious

00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:02.190
project. It was a very big movie about a real
life case that happened here in Chile about

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:08.150
some psychopaths. I actually shot 20 minutes
of the movie and turned it into some kind

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:16.280
of teaser and went to the contest that the
Government has to finance films and I didn’t

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:21.990
get it but I guess that was good because it
was a really big movie for me to take. I was

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:23.579
22 and really young.

00:01:23.579 --> 00:01:29.900
But I got the opportunity to work with great
talent like big talented actors and I had

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:36.890
a great script and that kind of opened up
something for me that when I finished the

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:45.799
university I went on to make and independent
short film which I did and went to a couple

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:56.110
of festivals and got a few awards and a year
after that I set out to make my first feature.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:02:07.439
I went to a lower scale of feature and in
the summer of 2013 I shot it. It’s called

00:02:07.439 --> 00:02:15.580
DISTANCE (DISTANCIA) and I premiered it in
2015.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:21.470
In the meantime when I was in post-production
of that film I switched cities and I came

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:27.780
to Santiago which is the capital of Chile
and started a job at University in a department

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:38.000
where I was in charge of making original content
where I developed a series of micro documentaries

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:45.140
and it has ten episodes, it’s about different
crafts that we are losing with time like shoemakers

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.990
and bicycle makers and stuff like that.

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:57.220
Then I made another project that consisted
of grabbing a few independent musicians from

00:02:57.220 --> 00:03:07.910
Chile (a couple of them songs from theirs)
and gave them to illustrators, painted a picture

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:16.060
of the song in a canvas and that became a
series of music videos that was along with

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:22.750
along with the museum exposition of the art
itself.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:33.660
And then I switched jobs and now I’m a director
of video content in big local radios from

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:42.330
Chile where I direct commercials and make
projects. Recently we premiered a documentary

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.120
about live music.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:53.270
I guess the problem current and always issue
about an independent filmmaker is making the

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:59.460
time to actually do the work to pull off your
own projects (it’s hard when you have a

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:06.090
day job). Luck for me my day job also consists
of on having a camera on hand and trying to

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:14.510
develop stories so I’m not that away from
the craft itself but it’s still an issue

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:20.880
to make time, to get home and sit down and
actually get something written down for a

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:25.080
project that you’re trying to pull off on
your own.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:35.880
Try not to let the day-to-day issues like
work, family, love life or whatever can come

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:44.030
up to get in the way of what you’re trying
to do because you actually have to be in a

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:50.440
very peaceful or sort of peaceful mindset
to actually get something done. It’s really

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:59.660
easy to start procrastinating and having a
lot of self-doubt and mainly issues with one’s

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:05.460
self like “I’m not good enough” or “Why
am I going to do this if it’s probably going

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.890
to turn out worse than the previous thing
that I did that didn’t actually turn out.”

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:15.690
But one is always it’s worse critic.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:23.690
I think that the best way that you can deal
with such issues is you can is be super headstrong

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:33.530
and try not to be a perfectionist…in my
case I’m always trying to achieve, I’m

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:39.410
a real perfectionist. So it’s kind of hard
when you are halfway through a project and

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:44.230
you realize that it’s not what you originally
had it in your mind and you don’t find it

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:51.470
perfect and don’t put it aside. It’s actually
the work itself that you need to keep doing

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:57.970
so I guess the way I’m trying to deal with
these issues is I’m trying to do it no matter

00:05:57.970 --> 00:06:02.260
what, no matter what the outcome is.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:09.500
Even if I’m young, I know a lot of young
filmmakers you have to do the work. You cannot

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:20.430
achieve an amount of greatness in the craft
if you don’t actually do it. So I guess

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:26.180
you always keep on learning while you’re
doing so it’s really important to just push

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:29.240
through it and keep doing no matter what.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:36.090
Don’t think about what’s coming next.
Once you get a job done and you put it out

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:45.560
there, it’s up to public opinion and public
opinion some time can but sometimes it doesn’t.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:51.890
But that doesn’t mean that it has to condemn
the way you do things, you just have to keep

00:06:51.890 --> 00:06:53.880
on doing them.

00:06:53.880 --> 00:07:03.260
I think what is actually paralyzing in some
way is fear first of all (fear of failure).

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:12.210
But you can’t event begin to fail if you
don’t try. So I guess that the hardest thing

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:20.230
for me to do is to be that headstrong and
just keep on doing it no matter what just

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:26.330
sit down and I don’t know finish the screenplay
even if it’s not going to get made, just

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:34.320
do it. Maybe have like 10-20 screenplays locked
in a drawer until there’s going to be just

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:38.981
probably one (could be a short film, could
be a documentary, could be anything). But

00:07:38.981 --> 00:07:44.190
one that will get shot and then eventually
is going to come up with other different issues

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:50.000
but you just have to finish every project
that you set your mind to do. I think that’s

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.410
really important because frustration is even
worse when you set out to start something

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:57.450
and you don’t finish it.

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:02.740
I don’t know if there are any filmmakers
out there that tend to deal with the same

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:11.300
issues that I do and I wonder what do you
do to overcome fear and keep pushing through?

